WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.420
Nvidia's really in its biggest
crisis in its three decade

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.500
history of operation.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.660
There's all the components
right now of a huge drama.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.800
In the last few days,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.900
Nvidia's CEO Jensen Huang has been at the

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.900
center of a firestorm.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.620
CEO Jensen Huang has admitted

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.780
to having an extramarital affair

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.560
after reports he was targeted
by a suspected Chinese spy.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.640
American officials suspect
she fed information to China

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.759
and also helped smuggle advanced
AI chips into the country.

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:30.020
Jensen Huang said to
have had a relationship

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.400
with an Nvidia executive
who reports to him.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.820
With these stunning revelations,

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.260
Huang's judgment is being
called into question

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.000
and he's now the focus of internal

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.900
and government investigations.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.340
But just a few weeks ago,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.760
things were looking very different.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.260
Hi, Jensen.
Hi Jensen.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.489
So this is... 

00:00:48.988 --> 00:00:50.353
Jensanity. 

00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:51.980
Thanks for having us on this trip.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.900
Welcome to Europe.
Kicking it off in Paris. 

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.020
In October,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.660
we followed NVIDIA's CEO
across Europe at the height

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.320
of his power, traveling to
four countries in four days.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.680
The leader of the leading
AI chip maker appeared

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.660
to be on a mission to sell
the world on a revolution

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.220
powered by Nvidia chips.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.460
We invested $8 billion in Europe,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.300
and so $8 billion worth of
jobs. It's a lot of jobs.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.680
The chip maker has poured billions

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.880
of dollars into startups,
frontier labs, and data centers,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.300
and Huang seemed like
a man defying gravity

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.380
until everything came crashing down.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.260
Jensen Huang is under investigation.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.860
Nvidia is in fact now
preparing to oust Jensen Huang

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.340
as the CEO of the company that he founded.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.000
Nvidia is Jensen and Jensen is Nvidia.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.660
Nvidia's board says it has no reason

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.040
to believe company secrets

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.520
or US national security were compromised.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.920
China calls the allegations a smear.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.920
I know the FBI's
looking into it in terms

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.140
of something else, and I think
that he'll be in good shape.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.760
But the episode has transformed the

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.400
meaning of our time with Huang.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.419
We're probably the only AI
company in the world that works

00:01:56.419 --> 00:01:58.140
with every AI company in the world.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.220
So at what point does

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.180
that dependence on one single company

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.039
become a systemic risk?

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:05.620
Well, they don't depend
on us. They choose us.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.700
What began as a window
into the massive influence

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.119
of a man at the peak now
looks more like a portrait

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:11.280
of concentrated power.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.740
It sent an entire industry into crisis

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.600
and raises tough questions about how much

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.360
that industry should depend on
one person and their company.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.120
How do you go to all these
cities? How do you keep up?

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.880
Like what are your travel must-haves?

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.480
Just pack my bags. I was up at midnight

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.480
and I've been working since.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.990
And here you are.

00:02:40.803 --> 00:02:42.580
Without dragging this out too long,

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.200
I would actually like to
welcome a surprise guest, 

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.200
Mr. Jensen Huang.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.840
On day one of his European
tour, Huang hyped up AI agents.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.680
This is really the beginning
now of AI becoming useful.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.880
We're seeing agents emerging in, in,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.660
in just about every single industry.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.040
And raffled off two of Nvidia's
new souped up AI computers

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.960
that retail for thousands of dollars each.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.420
Leaving the stage, he was
instantly mobbed by fans.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.460
Back up a little bit.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.180
It's my second thing to meet you.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:21.180
Can I take a picture?

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.013
Just a picture.

00:03:22.013 --> 00:03:23.920
So this is Jensanity.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.620
Everyone's trying to get
a picture with Jensen.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.180
Clearly it's very contagious.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.580
Excuse us, please.
We're heading this way.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:30.413
Excuse us

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.020
Huang was here to drum beat his vision

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.660
for a global AI ecosystem,
announcing investments

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.840
and partnerships at all
levels of the technology.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.660
France is jockeying to
become Europe's AI center.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.420
Noteworthy technical talent
has come out of the country,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.100
powering major companies
like Paris-based Mistral

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.640
and New York-based Hugging
Face, which Nvidia agreed

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.280
to acquire for $12.9 billion.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.499
Jensen likes to say AI is
like a five-layer cake.

00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:10.119
It's from the bottom up.
Energy, chips, infrastructure,

00:04:10.119 --> 00:04:13.220
the models, and the apps on top.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.520
Now, Nvidia started out
squarely in layer two,

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:19.199
making the chips, but
that has since changed.

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:21.080
And now Nvidia is investing up

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.280
and down all the layers
of the cake in power,

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.560
in data centers, in the
AI labs, in startups,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.440
which means no matter
how you slice it, more

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.280
and more of the AI industry
runs through Nvidia.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.760
You know what? Since
Emily's going to be filming,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.880
I want to put my whiskey down.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.200
In the last year alone, we
have invested $8 billion

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.980
in European startups.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.960
And in the next week, I'm going

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.960
to announce another two
more billion dollars.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.980
There's been a lot of talk that AI was

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.240
going to end humanity.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.940
You're saying it's a zero percent chance.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.560
It's just an engineering problem

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.540
and you got it solved.
It's what you're saying.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.740
No. What I say is that
it is in our control.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.680
Between now and the end of humanity,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.440
we're going to have a lot of work to do.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.520
Advancing capability, advancing
safety are not in conflict.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.260
They are in fact one.
Okay, you guys, I want

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.900
to thank all of you for coming.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.540
This is the beginning of AI in Europe.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.720
Let's go build it together,
okay? Thank you.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:30.720
Thank you.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.540
So there's more stuff
happening later tonight.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.420
He's going to be meeting
with government officials,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.300
other CEOs, other important
people in the tech

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.920
industry in France, and next up London.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:57.740
Estimates that the EU will need

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.500
to spend at least $3 trillion by 2035 just

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.740
to be truly self-sufficient in technology.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.040
Just got off the train in London

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.920
and I'm off to meet Jensen.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.980
27 years ago, NVIDIA went public

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.740
and they put me in this hotel.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.300
Oh, really?
Yes.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.420
It was the first time I ever had a butler.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:36.040
This was 2000 and you'll be blown away,

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:38.300
$300 million.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:43.180
Things have changed.
$300 million.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.540
That was a lot back then though.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:50.040
No, no, no, it was fine.
I was just happy to make it.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.960
You were such an
unlikely winner back then.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.560
Yeah, I was just happy
to stay in business.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.620
Didn't know what I was
doing. Didn't know how

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:58.800
to write a business plan.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.100
And now?

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.640
No, still don't know how
to write a business plan.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.680
What makes this trip a success?

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.860
Is it like government commitments,

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.100
selling chips, data centers?

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:16.002
I don't have a end game.

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:18.773
I don't have a roadmap.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.620
I don't have a plan. I'm
not trying to get anywhere.

00:07:24.192 --> 00:07:25.832
There's no completion.

00:07:25.832 --> 00:07:30.032
I'm kind of one of those
people that the journey,

00:07:30.213 --> 00:07:32.673
the journey is the destination.

00:07:32.673 --> 00:07:35.253
Huang's bio might make
you think otherwise.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.408
The highlight reel goes something like,

00:07:37.408 --> 00:07:40.547
Born in Taiwan in 1963, moved to the US

00:07:40.819 --> 00:07:43.100
and earned a national
ranking in table tennis.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:45.880
Famously loved his job
washing dishes at Denny's.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.860
Skipped a couple grades,
started college at 16 at Oregon

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.300
State, studied electrical engineering,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.860
became a chip designer
at competitor to be AMD.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.460
Then in 1993, NVIDIA was
born with humble beginnings.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:04.020
For $200, I bought 15%.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:05.020
I think it was 20% of NVIDIA.

00:08:06.019 --> 00:08:07.879
Huang and his two
business partners wanted

00:08:07.879 --> 00:08:11.140
to create chips, specifically
GPUs, that would make

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:14.960
for better video game graphics,
but it was a rocky start.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.159
In 2006, Nvidia unveiled CUDA, software

00:08:18.159 --> 00:08:21.080
that allowed its chips to do
more than run video games from

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.820
weather forecasting to gene sequencing.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.839
AI researchers soon discovered
those same chips could be

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.440
used to train their models.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.999
When word got back to
Huang, he went all in,

00:08:30.999 --> 00:08:33.460
betting big on tech built for AI

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.859
before most of the industry caught on.

00:08:35.859 --> 00:08:37.740
Nvidia powered the early breakthroughs

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.580
that brought AI mainstream,

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:40.920
and it's been one of the most lucrative

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.219
bets in corporate history.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.360
Today, you'll find Nvidia at the core

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.640
of most major tech companies,
OpenAI, Meta, Google,

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:54.380
Anthropic, Microsoft, SpaceX,
Amazon, and the list goes on.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.240
And Huang may be even bigger
than the company he runs,

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.060
parlaying influence into seats
at the world's most exclusive

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.480
tables and taking conveniently timed phone

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:03.620
calls from heads of state.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:07.083
Mr. President.

00:09:07.083 --> 00:09:09.077
You have President Trump on speed dial.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.420
What happens when your views
differ from the president's?

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:13.940
How do you walk that line?

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.080
We don't have to agree on anything.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.860
It's not my job to agree or disagree.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.900
It's my job to inform him of the decisions

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:25.720
that we make impact not
just the American citizens,

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.960
the prosperity of our
future, it impacts the world.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:35.740
And so if ever I have an
opportunity to help inform him so

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:37.940
that he could make a better decision,

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:40.780
whatever the decision is, whether
it serves me, whether it's

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.080
counter my interests, it's not my concern.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.160
It's only my concern that
I have an opportunity

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.560
to inform him, and that's it.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.580
100%, that's how I feel.

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.740
It's a who's who of leaders in the tech

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:55.940
and AI space who have
descended upon the White House.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.980
Doesn't really feel like
we're going to get any sort

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.120
of tangible way forward here when it comes

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.740
to the future of regulation.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.840
The main point here is that nobody wants

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.280
to cede competition
when it comes to China.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.340
In late September,
Huang was among a group

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.940
of tech titans called to
the White House for a summit

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.200
where lunch was served with
a side of super intelligence.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.560
We're using the word super

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.800
because the other word is a fake word.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.540
Huang stood alongside the
president as he rebranded AI

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:24.740
as super intelligence

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.220
and lauded a new White House Accord

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.800
that puts the tech industry in charge

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:31.080
of voluntarily policing itself
as it races to advance AI.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.100
You spent the last three
months trying to demystify AI.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.000
You said it's not a new species,
it's just a bunch of code.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.020
Isn't calling it super
the opposite of that?

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.520
Actually, I think calling it super is

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:44.680
the opposite of artificial.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.700
Super intelligence. Super intelligence.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.360
Super is the new word.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:52.420
Yeah. I do think it's
better than artificial.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:54.100
And I think President Trump's point is

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:55.780
that artificial just says fake.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:59.280
Well, everything's fake. We built it.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.080
And so I think at some
level, this is fake.

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.820
We're in a fake structure.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.660
It was built by humans,
but we don't say that.

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.480
It's a fake horse, otherwise known

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.360
as a car, but we don't say that.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.640
Is it super intelligence? Absolutely.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.080
I mean, it does a better
job researching than I do.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.880
It does a better job writing than I do.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.160
It's not super at everything that we do,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.420
but it's super at many things that we do.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.180
That day at the White
House, one critic said,

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:30.220
"The pledge he made them sign
was great for the cameras,

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:33.660
but utterly meaningless." The
people building the technology

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.260
are effectively helping to
write the rules that govern it.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:37.260
Is that appropriate?

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:39.840
I think it's very appropriate, and

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.120
the reason for that is this.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:44.840
Before we expect governments

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:49.440
and third parties to regulate
us, we should hold ourselves

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:51.440
to the highest possible responsibilities,

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:52.940
standards, accountability.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:55.660
The structure that was described
is very similar to the way

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.460
that safety systems are internally audited

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.860
and externally audited by many industries.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:03.460
Even if we would've had
a government regulation

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.000
or international government regulation,

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:10.960
who would do the auditing if
we didn't have the ecosystem

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.420
of companies that have
the capability to do so?

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:15.320
And so we have to go and
create that industry.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.600
We have to go find startups.

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:19.320
We have to encourage countries

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:23.260
and companies to get involved.

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.820
And so we've got a lot
of developing to do.

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.200
This call for development
is a familiar part

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.080
of Huang's message to the
world that all countries need

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:33.740
to build and control their own AI.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.580
He's popularizing a term
for this, sovereign AI.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:39.960
The realization that control

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.480
of data is power has
compelled other countries

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:44.980
to quickly develop their own
models and infrastructure.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:49.000
Establishing an independent
AI ecosystem entirely from

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.700
scratch would take decades.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.740
So Huang's pitch is each country
can simply build their own

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.260
capabilities on Nvidia,

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:58.480
which also means the more sovereign AI,

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.120
the more demand for NVIDIA chips.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:04.060
You're traveling across
Europe promoting this

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.720
idea of sovereign AI.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.960
Is it still sovereign if
it's built on American chips?

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:12.200
Yes. Sovereignty basically
means that you have

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:13.960
to control your own experience.

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.460
And in the case of AI, that
means control your own data.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:20.500
Your data, your life experience,
your company experience,

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:25.060
your country's culture, values,
the institutional knowledge

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:29.920
of your people, the flywheel
of creating more data,

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.680
more experience, more
intelligence, that belongs to you.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.980
Sovereignty means that
you have the ability

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:37.220
to determine your own destiny.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:39.120
You might need teamwork, you
might need collaboration,

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.280
might partnership and
support from other people.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.020
You could of course stand
on the shoulder of giants.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.340
You could start with
somebody else's technology,

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:46.760
open models, for example,

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.980
but then you could adapt
it and fine tune it.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.380
So I think in every
single way, open models,

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:58.340
Nvidia's technology, their own ecosystem,

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:00.740
their own data flywheel, their own agency,

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:01.880
their own aspirations and

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:03.920
hopes, that gives them sovereignty.

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:12.480
I understand we're in
the home of humanoids.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:14.720
Oh, I see a humanoid right there,

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.780
and there's a couple right in front of me.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.180
In London, Huang bounced
around between events,

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.480
handing out computers,
taking few, if any, breaks.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.480
All I've had today is a
quarter of a half of a sandwich.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:32.040
And going full Oprah
with startup capital.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.780
I'm investing in you. I'm investing in

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:35.720
you. I'm investing in you.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:36.960
While hammering on his message

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:39.560
that AI can be safe and controllable.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:41.720
And so if you're asking an
autonomous system to make you

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:47.080
an omelet, you don't have to
give it access to a chainsaw,

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:50.280
okay, or a shotgun.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:53.080
AI news was coming thick and
fast as Huang made the rounds.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:55.500
At one event, he gave a shout out

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.380
to London-based data center operator

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.460
Nscale, backed by Nvidia.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.280
I don't know. Is Josh
here? Is Nscale here?

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:05.300
You could be the most valuable UK startup

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:06.620
created in the last 10 years.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.520
But just a few days
later, investors got a case

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:15.440
of the AI build-out jitters

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.920
and were questioning Nscale's valuation.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.200
Microsoft pulled out of a deal

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:21.220
with the company citing
concerns about the cost

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:22.359
of electricity in the UK.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:27.439
UK's really the Goldilocks moment.

00:15:27.439 --> 00:15:30.879
You have every aspect
necessary to take advantage

00:15:30.879 --> 00:15:32.780
of this new industrial revolution,

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:35.039
and you just have to activate it.

00:15:35.039 --> 00:15:39.520
There's a strange general
behavior in the UK.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.380
If there was a million ways
to win, you find the one

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:44.659
reason not to do it.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:49.000
And I think you overthink things.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:52.000
As more countries develop
more powerful AI, does

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:55.020
that become harder to
police and align on safety?

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.940
Do we need something like a UN for AI?

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:01.360
Maybe. First of all,
dialogue needs to happen.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.080
As you know, with any movement,

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:07.400
it takes two people to start anything.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:09.120
And so I think every country wants

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:11.060
to be part of this dialogue.

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:13.940
How could any country not
be part of the dialogue

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:15.780
of intelligence?

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:17.480
Despite Huang's call to action,

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:20.880
international tensions over AI
development have been rising.

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:23.420
He's been pushing the White
House for favorable regulation

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:25.660
and playing both sides
of the AI arms race.

00:16:26.499 --> 00:16:29.240
Since AI advancement is limited
by how good the chips are,

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.660
NVIDIA's hardware is a crown
jewel for the United States,

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:35.340
technology that some leaders
say should be safeguarded

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:36.420
as a unique advantage.

00:16:37.379 --> 00:16:39.660
But Huang has been among the
loudest voices in Silicon

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:41.920
Valley, arguing that
limiting China's access

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:44.879
to US technology will only
make Beijing's domestic

00:16:44.879 --> 00:16:46.660
capability stronger in the long run.

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:51.740
The US approved selling
older NVIDIA chips to China,

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:53.239
but there are companies like DeepSeek

00:16:53.239 --> 00:16:56.740
that are prioritizing
building on Chinese hardware.

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:00.720
Is the promise of that
market slipping away at all

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.280
or shrinking at all?

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:04.480
Well, it's completely
blocked from us today.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.500
I did my best to inform

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:12.199
the policymakers in the United
States of the importance

00:17:12.199 --> 00:17:14.379
of engaging the Chinese market

00:17:14.379 --> 00:17:16.540
and making sure that we serve

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.779
that market for as long as we can.

00:17:18.779 --> 00:17:21.280
I think we made a mistake,

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:26.179
and this mistake is hard to
unwind, but I did my best.

00:17:26.179 --> 00:17:28.040
That's all I could do.

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:30.860
There are more Chinese AI researchers

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.660
than any other nation in the world,

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:37.900
and many of them working at the labs.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:41.120
I've been very clear that even though,

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:44.880
although we compete against
China, they're not our enemies.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.840
If we care deeply about the
safety of the world with respect

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:50.660
to artificial intelligence, then

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:53.140
of course we must open dialogues.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.780
We must work with them
in every possible way.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:59.840
A man talking to everyone
everywhere comes with risks,

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.220
something we discovered after the

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:02.400
recording of this interview.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.200
US Intelligence has reportedly
identified Alice Huang,

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.120
also known as Huang Lu, a
former television journalist,

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:12.260
as a spy who was recruited by Beijing

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:13.700
to target the Nvidia CEO.

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:17.220
She's an executive at
Megaspeed International, one

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:20.200
of the largest buyers of
NVIDIA chips in Southeast Asia,

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:21.520
which US officials suspect

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:23.220
of smuggling advanced chips to China.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:26.200
President Trump was
reportedly warned about their

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:27.480
relationship before flying

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:29.980
with Huang on Air Force
One in May to China.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:34.200
The Nvidia CEO initially said
the interactions were normal

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:37.440
and not unusual, but later
admitted to the affair.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.360
In a memo to employees,
Huang wrote, "I owe my family

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:42.480
and all of you an apology.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.140
I was unfaithful to my wife.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:46.500
My actions hurt her and our children.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.180
There is no excuse for what I did,

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.160
but this has turned into
something much bigger than an

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:55.180
infidelity scandal." Bloomberg
reported that in 2025,

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:57.740
Nvidia intervened in a US government probe

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.580
of suspected smuggling of
AI chips by Megaspeed.

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:03.660
Is there anything that China could do

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:05.540
or that could change in the relationship

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:06.720
between the United States and China

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:09.440
that would make you say
maybe we shouldn't sell

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:10.800
our chips to them?

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:12.160
Well, there are a lot of things that

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:13.920
they can do theoretically.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.360
They're pragmatic people.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.580
They want what's in the best interest of

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:19.380
their nation and their people.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:20.900
They want to be successful.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.260
United States want to be successful.

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:25.020
We want America to be rich.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.080
They want China to be rich,

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:29.580
and we should respect that aspiration.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:34.020
We should be comforted by nations

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:37.580
that want prosperity, that
want better for their people.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:40.840
It's the nations that
don't want prosperity,

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:43.900
don't care about what's in
the interest of their people.

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:46.580
Those are nations we
should be concerned about.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:48.600
One of the best things that
they've done, of course,

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:50.400
is lifted the opportunities

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:52.580
and the prosperity of hundreds of millions

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.700
of people on earth, and that's been great

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.620
for every country, including ours.

00:19:57.620 --> 00:19:59.720
But the AI race is a
struggle for dominance

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.400
with every player under enormous
pressure to move faster.

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:06.860
There are fears that the pace
could put humanity in danger.

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:10.580
OpenAI has disclosed
six new incidents of

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:13.420
what it calls AI model misalignment.

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:16.420
Anthropic says that its
Claude AI model went rogue,

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:18.040
carrying out unintended actions.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.600
To calm anxiety about rogue AI,

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:26.940
Nvidia released an open
source safety platform

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:30.760
of its own called OpenShell,
designed to govern AI agents,

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:32.520
even shut them down if necessary.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:36.240
But this announcement suggests
the current safety issues

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.440
require an engineering solution rather

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:39.500
than a regulatory one.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.660
AI is deeply unpopular
and people are scared.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:46.020
Sam Altman, Dario Amodei,

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:48.380
Elon Musk all say we should slow down.

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.960
You have been very
consistent in your optimism.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:53.700
What do you see that they don't?

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:56.320
You don't advance capability
leaving safety behind.

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:59.160
You don't advance safety
leaving capability behind.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:02.240
So let me give you an example.
When AI first came out,

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:04.760
the first criticism of
the original ChatGPT was,

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:05.840
of course, hallucination.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:07.480
It was just spewing nonsense.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:08.560
And so in order for us

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:11.800
to make these chatbots
grounded in truth, we have

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:14.820
to invent something like
retrieval augmented generation.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:16.540
We have to expand the context window.

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:20.040
We connect it to tools so
that it could do a search

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:23.060
before it answers the question,
knows when to do a search

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:26.000
because maybe the information requires

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.780
contextual information,
contemporary information,

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.960
and so it has to go out and do a search,

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:33.540
and all of that is part
of its conditioning

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:34.600
before it generates the answer.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:37.680
It also reasons afterwards.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.620
It reflects on the answer it gave you

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:43.240
and you go, "Is this the
best answer I could provide?"

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.740
Or it might even generate two

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:46.100
or three different versions of the answer

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:48.020
and reflects on which one's the best one.

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:49.920
All of that require technology.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:52.580
So safety and capability
to me is one idea.

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:56.760
Microsoft is calling
for an AI emergency brake

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:00.200
and says AI is less regulated
than washing machines.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:04.280
Anthropic is saying their
models could evade shutdown

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:05.300
in a matter of months.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.640
What would you have to see
that might convince you

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:11.500
that the industry should
slow down or stop?

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:15.960
Several things. One, if we're
developing agentic systems

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:20.900
or autonomous systems,
the agents have to be in

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:23.700
rigorous containment systems,

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:28.880
and those agents must be
monitored, just job one.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:31.380
If you're not able to do
that, you should not test it.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.240
If you're not able to do that,
you should not deploy it.

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:37.160
Well, these models that went
rogue are unreleased models.

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:38.480
So is that reason

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:39.960
that they should shut
down, shut the labs down?

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:44.160
No. What they should do
is that they should contain

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:46.540
and monitor those experiments.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.500
But they say they can't contain them.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:52.540
No, that's just made up.
Nonsense. I cannot be proven.

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:54.820
You can't prove that to me. Yeah,

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.960
you can absolutely contain,
you can absolutely monitor.

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:02.500
Until you try to do it, don't
tell me you can't do it.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:04.400
And so I'm just not
going to listen to that.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:05.600
So are you saying that you don't

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:07.160
believe what they're saying?

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:10.980
I don't know all that they're saying.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.660
If you're developing agentic systems

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:14.620
and you can't monitor
it, you should not do so.

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:15.620
That's just the bottom line.

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:19.880
Even the biggest AI optimists see risks.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:25.920
They also see solutions. What
is the biggest risk you see?

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:29.860
Developing agentic systems
without containment...

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:34.100
Developing agentic systems
without monitoring,

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:36.180
it is extremely high risk.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:40.720
You should not do that. And
until you have the ability

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:42.780
to contain and monitor,

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:45.460
you should not run
large scale experiments.

00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:47.660
It just sounds like
that's already happening.

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:51.340
Then you got to stop.
It's really simple.

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:53.700
So that would be the
message to Anthropic

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.120
to OpenAI where we've seen these -

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:56.980
You should contain your technology,

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:58.020
contain your experiments,

00:23:58.020 --> 00:23:59.340
you should monitor your experiments.

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:02.820
You should give these agents,

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:05.340
when you deploy them, minimum rights.

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:07.300
They should be treated with zero trust.

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:09.220
They should be monitored all the time.

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:11.140
If one of the labs was doing something

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:13.240
that was too dangerous, that
you felt was too dangerous,

00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:14.620
would Nvidia stop selling to them?

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:24.020
Well, it's a hypothetical,
but of course. Yeah.

00:24:24.020 --> 00:24:25.860
Yeah, it's a hypothetical, but of course,

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:27.760
we don't run other people's companies,

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:32.760
and there's a limit
to the judgment by which

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:35.860
we apply to other companies'
use of our technology,

00:24:35.860 --> 00:24:37.540
our technology's general purpose.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:41.080
But if we know for certain
that an application

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:43.420
of our technology is bringing
harm to people, of course.

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:53.580
Get into Thursday. Signs

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:56.660
of distress are emerging in
leveraged AI computing firms

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:59.600
credit jitters sink chip stocks
on a six-day losing streak,

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.900
the longest run of the year so far.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.840
And now we're in Stockholm.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:07.280
This is a city known for
its canals and its islands,

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:08.920
but it's also a thriving tech hub.

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:12.860
AI, of course, is a bigger
and bigger part of that,

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:15.420
and much of it is being powered by NVIDIA.

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:24.500
Our guest of honor needs
little introduction. Welcome.

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:26.200
Welcome to Stockholm.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:27.360
Huang headlined a kickoff

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.340
for Sweden's Nvidia-backed
sovereign AI supercomputer,

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:33.380
attended by tech luminaries like Spotify

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:34.540
Co-Founder Daniel Ek.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:38.600
We have tremendous educational system

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:42.260
that produces incredible
talent across many different

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:44.060
disciplines and industries.

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:46.680
I don't think that there's
a problem of potential.

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:49.000
I think what we do need
is to speed things up

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.120
and we need to be more ambitious.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:56.280
Meanwhile, more tough
AI news was coming in.

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:58.960
Data center company
CoreWeave, backed by Nvidia,

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:00.840
was taking a beating in the market over

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:02.940
concerns about its debt load.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:06.540
A seemingly unfazed Huang told
reporters CoreWeave's strength

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:08.360
is improving every day,

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.720
but other AI stocks were
selling off too, as markets seem

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:14.040
to be questioning the
economics of the AI boom.

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:18.140
More than a hundred data center
projects have been blocked

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.280
or delayed in the US
just so far this year.

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:22.180
There's pushback in Europe too.

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:26.540
You have AI IPOs getting
delayed, getting scrapped.

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:29.180
Are we seeing cracks in the AI boom,

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:31.420
and could you be left holding the bag?

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:32.660
It's too fundamental. I think

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:34.440
this is the future of computing.

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:38.220
At the fundamental level,
computing will never go back.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:41.460
We used to have computers
that the software is written

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.720
by hand and the computer's
waiting for us to use it.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:50.020
In the future and where we
are now, computers are able

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:51.320
to learn environments

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.180
and problems that are too complicated

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:55.000
for any human to program.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:58.220
The size of it, the way that
computers are used instead

00:26:58.220 --> 00:26:59.200
of a tool, it's not an

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:01.020
infrastructure, that's
never going to go back.

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:02.840
The capabilities of AI now that we know

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:04.740
what it can do, that's
never going to go back.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:08.840
So I think in a lot many ways
it's fundamental irrespective

00:27:08.840 --> 00:27:10.220
of near-term challenges.

00:27:10.220 --> 00:27:13.460
And so I think the narrative
of the industry is really out

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:14.880
of control and ought to be stopped,

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:19.780
but that's just an industry growing up.

00:27:19.780 --> 00:27:21.820
You probably remember the
good old days of the internet

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:23.880
and the industry grew up.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:28.380
And I think that our industry
is many of the young companies

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:30.400
are going through their growing pains

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:32.940
and they're growing, of
course, incredibly fast.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:35.960
There's an accountability
responsibility that goes along

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:37.280
with building companies that I think

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:38.460
they're just growing into.

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:42.380
Nvidia keeps adding new
products, buying up companies.

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:45.100
In some cases, you're competing
with your own customers.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:47.680
Why do you need to have such
a broad range of offerings?

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:51.120
We do as little as we can.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:54.300
It seems like you're doing a lot.

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:56.740
Surprisingly little. I
think we've done a good job

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:02.100
generalizing the work that we
do so that it could be applied

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:03.800
to many different things, not

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:05.540
because we're doing many
different things, it's

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:07.560
because we generalized one thing.

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:09.760
And so if you look at our architecture,

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.320
we're the only architecture
that's general purpose,

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:15.260
meaning we run every closed
model, we run every open model,

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:17.780
we run biology models to language models,

00:28:17.780 --> 00:28:19.840
auto-regressive models,
the transformer models,

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:21.760
the diffusion models, and all the

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:22.980
different various technologies.

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:26.300
The challenge of generalization
is, of course, performance.

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:30.560
How can you be the world's
fastest F1 race car

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:34.120
and also be a really great sedan?

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:36.240
And so that's the miracle

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:38.200
of the technology that we've created.

00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:40.560
On the one hand, we're the
world's fastest F1 racing cars,

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:43.360
and we're the fastest computers,

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:45.520
and we're always at the
top of the leaderboard.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.940
You benchmark us, we're always the best.

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:51.340
On the other hand, because we're fungible,

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:52.800
we're also the lowest cost.

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:55.080
Nvidia recently committed

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:58.080
to spending about half a trillion
dollars across the global

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:01.760
AI industry, and that
number is quickly climbing.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:03.480
There's concern that in doing so,

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:05.900
Nvidia is creating false demand,

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:07.940
giving companies the money
they need to turn around

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:10.700
and buy products from Nvidia.

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:12.900
It's no secret that
critics have accused Nvidia

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.640
of circular financing.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:18.140
How do you toe the line between
boosting promising startups

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:19.620
and blowing up a bubble?

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:21.320
If AI is such a gold mine, why do you have

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:22.960
to underwrite so much of it?

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:25.560
Yeah, this technology
requires high CapEx.

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:29.300
If you look at this generation
of AI startups, it's very,

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:32.840
very rare that you find a
company that can be funded on $10

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:36.340
million, and it's not
unusual to find a company

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:37.880
that requires $10 billion.

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.680
And the reason for that is
because it needs this computation

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:43.920
resource in order to
deliver the intelligence.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:48.920
Some of the highest quality
companies in the AI ecosystem

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:53.080
across each one of the layers
of the five-layer cake,

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:57.300
it connects us to the best and
the most talented companies.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:00.440
It helps us continue to advance Nvidia's

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:01.560
platform, if you will,

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:03.780
because these companies are
going to be built on Nvidia's

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:06.980
technology, and it helps us capitalize

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.540
and be one of the early
investors in many of them.

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:13.860
We have a highly disciplined
approach of making sure

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.880
that we are, although one
of the earliest investors,

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:19.000
and many times even the anchor investor,

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:20.160
we're not the lead investor.

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:22.280
We're not the largest investor.

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:27.060
In all cases, we own less
than 20% of the company.

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:30.340
We've invested some, I guess,
in the last five years,

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:33.080
maybe close to $100 billion
or something like that.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:37.300
That hundred billion
dollars resulted in, well,

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:39.980
at least five times as much other

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.780
institutional investors
into this ecosystem.

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:46.660
Most of the world's leading
AI companies depend on Nvidia,

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:48.720
not just your hardware,
but your software as well.

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:50.740
We're probably the only AI
company in the world that works

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.460
with every AI company in the world.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:53.540
So at what point does

00:30:53.540 --> 00:30:55.500
that dependence on one single company

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:57.360
become a systemic risk?

00:30:57.360 --> 00:30:59.840
Well, they don't depend
on us, they choose us.

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:01.600
I don't worry about that.
And the reason for that is

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:04.760
because we don't take
our position for granted.

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:09.220
We came from nothing and we
have to earn our keep every day.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:12.120
We have competitors in
every company, every

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:13.300
industry, every country.

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:16.980
With respect to the size of the market,

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:18.460
just remember, it's just a number.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:26.620
Nvidia can only be as large
as the, if you will, the pond

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:28.640
that we're in, the market that we're in.

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:32.180
But when somebody asks me,
America needs to win the AI race,

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:34.980
what I hear is not that one or two

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:37.200
or three companies wins the AI race.

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:39.980
What I mean is America's
companies win the AI race.

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:42.700
If we do the opposite, we scare people

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:45.300
and we cause people anxiety,
then America will lose.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:48.160
In a new administration,

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:52.720
will NVIDIA's dominance be
at risk? Are you prepared?

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:54.740
Are we prepared? Yeah, sure.

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:58.740
I'm in service of our
policymakers and our leaders

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:00.120
and whatever they want
to talk to me about.

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:04.520
I'm more than happy to
share with them what I know.

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:05.820
I know a lot about this technology.

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:07.940
I know a lot about engineering

00:32:07.940 --> 00:32:09.420
and I know what it takes to build this

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:11.540
and I know what we're doing to build it.

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:16.020
And if you ask me my thoughts on

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:21.020
whether we can advance AI
with prosperity for the future

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:23.600
while doing so every single
step of the way safely

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:26.440
and responsibly, I have
absolutely zero concern

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:28.880
that we have the ability to do so.

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:31.320
Now, whether we would do so is
a completely different thing,

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:32.800
but we have the technology to do so.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:35.180
You said AI supply

00:32:35.180 --> 00:32:38.340
and demand will balance out
in maybe five to 10 years.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:42.220
What happens when the world
doesn't need as many new chips?

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:45.180
New chips will replace
the old chips, just kind

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:47.100
of like the personal computer industry.

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:49.160
We got, what, a billion
users in the world today?

00:32:50.361 --> 00:32:53.361
In 1993 when NVIDIA was first founded,

00:32:53.361 --> 00:32:54.421
the personal computer industry

00:32:54.421 --> 00:32:56.441
was doubling every single year.

00:32:56.441 --> 00:32:58.641
And of course it doesn't double anymore.

00:32:58.641 --> 00:33:00.621
At the moment, we're kind
of doubling every year.

00:33:00.621 --> 00:33:03.581
And my sense is that we're
going to keep doing that

00:33:03.581 --> 00:33:05.961
as we build out the
infrastructure of the world.

00:33:05.961 --> 00:33:07.761
And how far out can
you see that doubling?

00:33:08.801 --> 00:33:10.581
We're going to do that for several years

00:33:10.581 --> 00:33:12.901
and then we'll reevaluate.

00:33:12.901 --> 00:33:14.161
And so we're kind of two years into

00:33:14.161 --> 00:33:15.720
an infrastructure build out.

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:19.221
And if you look at
previous technology cycles,

00:33:20.321 --> 00:33:21.401
it takes about 10 years

00:33:21.401 --> 00:33:24.421
or so to reach some kind of maturity.

00:33:24.421 --> 00:33:26.661
And then after that, you're
in replacement cycles.

00:33:26.661 --> 00:33:28.961
And so did that with phones,
did that with the internet,

00:33:28.961 --> 00:33:29.981
did that with PCs.

00:33:30.881 --> 00:33:32.681
I think it'll happen here too.

00:33:32.681 --> 00:33:34.901
Could there be a breakthrough
that means AI is powered

00:33:34.901 --> 00:33:37.121
by a fraction of the energy?

00:33:37.121 --> 00:33:38.341
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.
And

00:33:38.341 --> 00:33:40.001
then what does that mean for Nvidia?

00:33:40.001 --> 00:33:42.241
No, we're just going to keep
making things more efficient.

00:33:44.301 --> 00:33:46.881
There's a weird dynamic.
People used to tell me, "Hey,

00:33:46.881 --> 00:33:48.141
Jensen, if you make your chips

00:33:48.141 --> 00:33:51.361
that much better every
generation, doesn't it say

00:33:51.361 --> 00:33:54.381
that customers just buy less?" And

00:33:54.381 --> 00:33:56.081
that same exact logic goes,

00:33:57.061 --> 00:33:59.521
if we make companies more productive,

00:33:59.521 --> 00:34:00.981
don't we hire less people?

00:34:02.481 --> 00:34:05.281
And there's this weird
thing called ambition.

00:34:05.281 --> 00:34:09.081
If you gave me 36 hours a day,
I'm not going to sleep more

00:34:10.801 --> 00:34:14.301
because I've got ideas,
I have things to do.

00:34:14.301 --> 00:34:15.701
We'll end up doing more. And

00:34:15.701 --> 00:34:17.521
so if AI makes us all more

00:34:17.521 --> 00:34:19.801
intelligent, I become more ambitious.

00:34:19.801 --> 00:34:22.641
If the cost of doing something
becomes marginally close

00:34:22.641 --> 00:34:27.401
to zero, it turns out that
resource becomes widely abundant.

00:34:27.401 --> 00:34:29.521
I think the economy's going to get larger.

00:34:29.521 --> 00:34:31.281
I think the industries will get larger.

00:34:31.281 --> 00:34:34.181
I think the semiconductor
industry will be, at this point,

00:34:34.181 --> 00:34:36.341
very clearly one of the largest
industries in the world.

00:34:40.861 --> 00:34:43.221
Chip makers set for their
longest losing streak since

00:34:43.221 --> 00:34:45.741
2008, leaving investors questioning the

00:34:45.741 --> 00:34:48.161
prospects for the AI trade.

00:34:48.161 --> 00:34:50.221
Finally, we're in Berlin.

00:34:50.221 --> 00:34:52.281
This is where Nvidia is holding its third

00:34:52.281 --> 00:34:53.501
big conference of the year.

00:34:54.481 --> 00:34:57.961
Germany built its economy on
the last industrial revolution,

00:34:57.961 --> 00:35:00.561
but the economy's been
having kind of a rough time.

00:35:01.601 --> 00:35:05.741
Jensen likes to say AI is the
new industrial revolution,

00:35:05.741 --> 00:35:07.481
and if anyone could use it, it's here.

00:35:09.261 --> 00:35:13.181
Germany is rich with the
most important part of AI,

00:35:13.181 --> 00:35:16.721
which is the science, the
math, the computer scientists,

00:35:16.721 --> 00:35:19.841
the physical AI, the deep
science capabilities.

00:35:19.841 --> 00:35:21.481
You have the spirit of innovation,

00:35:21.481 --> 00:35:23.441
you have the spirit of entrepreneurship.

00:35:23.441 --> 00:35:26.821
We now have to find a way
to get this flywheel going

00:35:26.821 --> 00:35:28.241
and we have to start by building the

00:35:28.241 --> 00:35:29.561
computers, the infrastructure.

00:35:31.161 --> 00:35:32.361
In Berlin, the line

00:35:32.361 --> 00:35:35.021
to see Huang was out the
door and around the block.

00:35:35.021 --> 00:35:36.781
The event was held at Saturn,

00:35:36.781 --> 00:35:39.582
which is like a German
Best Buy on steroids.

00:35:41.962 --> 00:35:46.562
Jensen is in the building and he's here.

00:35:47.582 --> 00:35:50.142
He's here to see everybody
tonight. Hey everybody.

00:35:52.602 --> 00:35:53.922
Hello,
Berlin.

00:35:55.402 --> 00:35:58.582
How do you say Saturn? Satoorn.

00:35:58.582 --> 00:36:01.202
Everything in German, you
got to say it really serious.

00:36:02.362 --> 00:36:03.362
Satoorn.

00:36:06.122 --> 00:36:07.962
Obviously you've had this
big week, you've gone to

00:36:07.962 --> 00:36:10.222
so many countries, so much exciting stuff,

00:36:10.222 --> 00:36:12.942
but at the same time
there's constant headlines,

00:36:12.942 --> 00:36:16.522
this massive market sell off,
chip stocks under pressure.

00:36:16.522 --> 00:36:17.822
As this trip has been going on,

00:36:17.822 --> 00:36:19.282
what have you been
watching in the markets?

00:36:19.282 --> 00:36:21.502
What's your take on what's
happening with CoreWeave?

00:36:21.502 --> 00:36:22.962
All of this is going to pass and the

00:36:22.962 --> 00:36:24.502
reason for that is very simple.

00:36:24.502 --> 00:36:27.102
We're going through a
new platform transition.

00:36:27.102 --> 00:36:29.382
News, as you know, happens all the time,

00:36:29.382 --> 00:36:31.242
but it won't change the fundamentals.

00:36:33.642 --> 00:36:36.142
You've said AI will create
more jobs than it destroys.

00:36:36.142 --> 00:36:37.342
AI does create more jobs.

00:36:37.342 --> 00:36:39.482
You've said people worried
about losing their jobs

00:36:39.482 --> 00:36:41.182
should learn AI.

00:36:41.182 --> 00:36:43.462
What do you say to the
person who has learned AI,

00:36:43.462 --> 00:36:44.642
who is doing exactly that

00:36:44.642 --> 00:36:46.522
but still watching their job slip away?

00:36:47.942 --> 00:36:50.562
Well, first of all, I think
you're asking a theoretical

00:36:50.562 --> 00:36:54.642
question, but let's apply
the practical answer first.

00:36:54.642 --> 00:36:57.962
Now remember, AI is unlike previous

00:36:57.962 --> 00:37:01.442
technologies in the sense that
this requires manufacturing.

00:37:01.442 --> 00:37:02.842
It's creating construction jobs,

00:37:02.842 --> 00:37:04.522
electrician jobs, plumbing jobs.

00:37:04.522 --> 00:37:07.486
In the last six months,
something like

00:37:07.486 --> 00:37:11.224
$400 to $600 billion dollars,

00:37:11.224 --> 00:37:13.580
were invested into AI startups.

00:37:13.642 --> 00:37:16.002
And the reason for that is
people see AI is productive,

00:37:16.002 --> 00:37:17.842
it's effective and people
want to invest in it.

00:37:18.762 --> 00:37:21.342
Those four to 600 billion dollars

00:37:21.342 --> 00:37:23.022
has just created how many jobs?

00:37:23.022 --> 00:37:25.622
And so of course, this
technology is a new tool

00:37:26.602 --> 00:37:28.162
and this new tool is

00:37:28.162 --> 00:37:31.882
therefore going to cause
everybody's jobs to change.

00:37:31.882 --> 00:37:34.842
Resisting the change, it
won't make it go away.

00:37:34.842 --> 00:37:37.942
The superpowers that the tech
companies have are now made

00:37:37.942 --> 00:37:39.942
available to literally everybody.

00:37:39.942 --> 00:37:42.242
It democratized the technology,

00:37:42.242 --> 00:37:44.962
it's elevated everybody's capabilities.

00:37:44.962 --> 00:37:47.582
And so I see the benefits of AI.

00:37:48.602 --> 00:37:51.142
I'm obviously very aware of the risks

00:37:51.142 --> 00:37:54.422
and the technology risks,
but in the final analysis,

00:37:54.422 --> 00:37:56.522
if I don't see the optimistic,

00:37:56.522 --> 00:38:00.622
if we don't see the optimistic,
incredibly wonderful future

00:38:00.622 --> 00:38:02.102
that we're going to create, for

00:38:02.102 --> 00:38:03.122
what reason are we building it?

00:38:08.002 --> 00:38:10.882
The final act in this dash
across Europe was Nvidia's own

00:38:10.882 --> 00:38:12.462
flagship conference, GTC.

00:38:15.402 --> 00:38:19.122
Welcome to the stage. Nvidia
founder and CEO, Jensen Huang.

00:38:19.122 --> 00:38:23.242
Hello, Berlin. Hello, Berlin.

00:38:23.242 --> 00:38:25.622
The CEO stuck to his bullish pitch,

00:38:25.622 --> 00:38:27.502
recapping his new European investments

00:38:27.502 --> 00:38:30.303
and partnerships,
announcing new AI security

00:38:30.303 --> 00:38:31.783
and data center products

00:38:31.783 --> 00:38:34.303
and playing around with
some AI trained robots.

00:38:34.303 --> 00:38:36.403
What did we talk about?
We talked about Europe.

00:38:36.403 --> 00:38:39.643
Pay attention. Stay focused. Come here.

00:38:42.283 --> 00:38:43.703
At one point, he was interrupted

00:38:43.703 --> 00:38:45.383
by a protest over the reported use

00:38:45.383 --> 00:38:47.223
of Nvidia chips in Russian drones.

00:38:50.003 --> 00:38:51.183
Thank you. Have
a great GTC.

00:38:54.983 --> 00:38:57.983
You said 0% chance AI
ends the world by 2030.

00:38:57.983 --> 00:38:59.763
What about beyond that, 2040?

00:38:59.763 --> 00:39:02.163
How far into the future are you so sure?

00:39:02.163 --> 00:39:04.063
I'm quite sure. I'm quite sure.

00:39:05.563 --> 00:39:08.463
I'm quite confident in
the agency of people.

00:39:10.003 --> 00:39:13.303
I'm quite confident that today
is a safer world than it was

00:39:13.303 --> 00:39:15.143
100 years ago, 50 years ago,

00:39:15.143 --> 00:39:17.503
100 years ago, and 250 years ago.

00:39:17.503 --> 00:39:20.003
I'm quite confident in that
because the facts would show it.

00:39:21.183 --> 00:39:25.683
Although the news headlines
are more pessimistic

00:39:25.683 --> 00:39:28.603
and more bad news than
ever, but that's just

00:39:28.603 --> 00:39:30.903
because bad news is easier to sell.

00:39:30.903 --> 00:39:33.043
Many people appreciate your optimism

00:39:33.043 --> 00:39:34.123
and get excited by it,

00:39:35.123 --> 00:39:39.143
but what if your optimism
proves wrong in the end?

00:39:39.143 --> 00:39:41.363
Then I can say that I
did everything I could.

00:39:41.363 --> 00:39:44.243
And the reason for that is
because not only am I optimistic,

00:39:45.363 --> 00:39:47.043
I'm doing everything I can

00:39:47.043 --> 00:39:48.783
to create the technology necessary

00:39:48.783 --> 00:39:51.783
to keep the industry safe, secure.

00:39:51.783 --> 00:39:55.563
And so let's not talk about the future

00:39:56.403 --> 00:39:58.643
in a pessimistic way while
we do nothing about it

00:39:58.643 --> 00:40:00.583
to prevent that from happening.

00:40:00.583 --> 00:40:04.423
And so maybe I'm older, I'm more parental.

00:40:05.303 --> 00:40:08.723
Don't just deliver the bad news.

00:40:08.723 --> 00:40:10.683
Tell me what you're going to do about it.

00:40:10.683 --> 00:40:15.683
And so long as we can prove
to ourselves that we're doing

00:40:15.843 --> 00:40:16.843
both sides of that coin,

00:40:18.123 --> 00:40:20.263
the optimistic future while safeguarding

00:40:22.123 --> 00:40:23.723
our way in doing so.

00:40:23.723 --> 00:40:26.203
So long as we do both sides
of that, I'm perfectly fine.

00:40:27.523 --> 00:40:30.643
After a whirlwind week of
AI pomp, one thing was clear.

00:40:30.643 --> 00:40:33.023
Jensen Huang has been
the leading evangelist

00:40:33.023 --> 00:40:36.423
for an extraordinary new age
of artificial intelligence.

00:40:36.423 --> 00:40:38.223
Nvidia, the US economy,

00:40:38.223 --> 00:40:41.223
maybe even the entire global
financial system might just

00:40:41.223 --> 00:40:43.823
rest on his optimism and accomplishments.

00:40:43.823 --> 00:40:46.623
But now Huang himself is under scrutiny.

00:40:46.623 --> 00:40:49.403
He has led the business since
its very beginning 33 years

00:40:49.403 --> 00:40:51.783
ago, and even as Nvidia grew into one

00:40:51.783 --> 00:40:53.443
of the world's most powerful companies,

00:40:53.443 --> 00:40:56.563
there's still no one designated
to potentially replace him.

00:40:56.563 --> 00:40:59.063
And the recent allegations
show just how tenuous it is

00:40:59.063 --> 00:41:02.523
to pin the hope and future
of an industry on one person.

00:41:02.523 --> 00:41:05.903
You famously run Nvidia in a
very unconventional way, lots

00:41:05.903 --> 00:41:08.563
of direct reports, no meetings.

00:41:08.563 --> 00:41:10.943
As the company has exploded in size

00:41:10.943 --> 00:41:13.583
and the problems have become more complex,

00:41:13.583 --> 00:41:18.023
how has your management style
and your mentality changed?

00:41:18.023 --> 00:41:19.784
Well, my management style is

00:41:19.784 --> 00:41:21.304
what made it possible
for the company to grow.

00:41:22.264 --> 00:41:24.804
Our organization is
what made the company to

00:41:24.804 --> 00:41:26.944
thrive and innovate.

00:41:26.944 --> 00:41:29.084
I wanted to create an
architecture of a company with

00:41:29.084 --> 00:41:32.224
as few silos as possible.

00:41:32.224 --> 00:41:36.324
The problem with silos, if the
organizations are too rigid,

00:41:37.824 --> 00:41:41.184
then the company has very
difficult time adapting to change.

00:41:41.184 --> 00:41:43.924
So I think a modern company
really wants to be more

00:41:43.924 --> 00:41:47.384
and more agile and less
and less structured.

00:41:47.384 --> 00:41:49.484
What about the CEO
who runs that company?

00:41:49.484 --> 00:41:52.604
You should be as mentally
flexible as possible.

00:41:52.604 --> 00:41:54.804
I think you have to hold
yourself responsible.

00:41:54.804 --> 00:41:56.144
You have agency. You're the CEO

00:41:56.144 --> 00:41:57.484
of the company for God's sakes.

00:41:57.484 --> 00:41:59.924
You have to have high
standards and expectations.

00:41:59.924 --> 00:42:02.524
Your son and your daughter
are working at Nvidia.

00:42:02.524 --> 00:42:04.524
I think your son-in-law
also works at Nvidia.

00:42:04.524 --> 00:42:06.384
Robotics, yeah.
There is talk

00:42:06.384 --> 00:42:08.284
that your daughter is in
line to be your successor.

00:42:08.284 --> 00:42:09.284
Is that true?

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:12.764
Well, first of all, I
don't hope the job on her.

00:42:13.824 --> 00:42:15.164
And the reason for that is

00:42:15.164 --> 00:42:18.144
because that's their aspiration, Spencer

00:42:18.144 --> 00:42:21.044
or Madison's, they have to go earn it.

00:42:21.044 --> 00:42:22.864
And it's really, really hard work.

00:42:22.864 --> 00:42:26.784
It takes a lot of
sacrifice for many people.

00:42:26.784 --> 00:42:29.484
They can focus on their
job and their families.

00:42:29.484 --> 00:42:31.884
When you're the CEO of a
company, you have to focus on,

00:42:31.884 --> 00:42:32.944
of course, your family,

00:42:33.824 --> 00:42:36.824
but I've got a whole bunch of
other families I'm caring for.

00:42:36.824 --> 00:42:38.444
I'm responsible for an entire industry.

00:42:39.544 --> 00:42:43.784
And so I think the
pressure of being the CEO

00:42:43.784 --> 00:42:45.724
is high and it's supposed to be high.

00:42:47.604 --> 00:42:49.324
It should not be an easy job.

00:42:49.324 --> 00:42:51.524
It shouldn't be a job
that you take for granted.

00:42:51.524 --> 00:42:55.944
When I decided to be CEO, I
didn't realize the magnitude

00:42:55.944 --> 00:42:58.204
of the pressure and responsibilities,

00:42:58.204 --> 00:43:01.544
but look, that's what I signed up for.

00:43:01.544 --> 00:43:04.264
And so now that my family

00:43:04.264 --> 00:43:08.284
and all of the people that are near me see

00:43:09.244 --> 00:43:10.524
the responsibilities of the job

00:43:10.524 --> 00:43:13.084
and what it takes, then they just got

00:43:13.084 --> 00:43:16.204
to ask themselves whether
this is the level of sacrifice

00:43:16.204 --> 00:43:17.724
and responsibility you want.

00:43:17.724 --> 00:43:20.204
And I'm really hoping that the next CEO

00:43:20.204 --> 00:43:21.504
of the company's already at the company,

00:43:23.284 --> 00:43:25.604
but 20 years from now,
I hope I stay healthy

00:43:25.604 --> 00:43:28.484
and I hope I continue to earn my keep,

00:43:29.584 --> 00:43:33.124
but so long as I can do that,
20 years from now, I hope

00:43:33.964 --> 00:43:36.404
they're already here,
but they have to earn it.

00:43:36.404 --> 00:43:38.664
For the next generation
of leaders at Nvidia,

00:43:38.664 --> 00:43:41.464
what will their challenge look like?

00:43:41.464 --> 00:43:43.484
I don't know. And that's the beauty.

00:43:43.484 --> 00:43:45.084
In fact, that's the
exactly the right answer.

00:43:45.084 --> 00:43:46.564
We should create the conditions

00:43:46.564 --> 00:43:49.264
by which the company continues to thrive,

00:43:49.264 --> 00:43:52.884
and we should create the
conditions by which the leaders

00:43:52.884 --> 00:43:55.064
of our company continues to grow

00:43:55.064 --> 00:43:56.184
and that the next leader

00:43:56.184 --> 00:43:57.664
of the company is already at NVIDIA.

00:43:57.664 --> 00:43:59.844
That's my job is to create that condition.

00:43:59.844 --> 00:44:03.724
And just as I can be fired,
the next CEO has to be hired.

00:44:03.724 --> 00:44:08.505
And I like it that way. I like that.

00:44:08.505 --> 00:44:13.505
I like that we can't be overly
complacent about our position

00:44:13.605 --> 00:44:16.205
inside the company and that
we can't take it for granted.

00:44:16.205 --> 00:44:20.185
And you're always vulnerable,
you're always earning it,

00:44:20.185 --> 00:44:22.965
you're always striving to be better.

00:44:22.965 --> 00:44:23.905
That's a condition that's good.

